me that's  getting into the interview room do you  want me to continue  so if you're selected to interview  a lot of the interviews are open-ended  so the interviewer will sit down just  like we did and in have an open-ended  conversation for the first five or so  minutes  why are you here what do you like to do  in your spare time and a feeling of  personal chemistry often based on  similarity in things in terms of things  that are outside of work outside of  school are a huge source of interview  success  after that some firms will  have some sort of skills test in  consulting it's the most structured you  do what's called a case interview and i  can tell you more about that if you're  interested but the idea is you're  supposed to  speak to your interviewer in a way where  you are equals  throughout the interview that you're  supposed to foster a feeling of  chemistry and all of these things  success is partly dependent upon having  a baseline familiarity with upper middle  class and upper class culture  you say in your book that a structured  interview benefits  the non-elites more than the elites is  that what do you mean for the most part  so structured interviews let me start  with unstructured interviews  unstructured interviews are the most  common interviews that american hiring  managers use they love them because it  allows your personal theory of success  to to be the guiding force you may think  that what really drives success in life  and in your job is the fact that you're  from a certain state or that you come  from a certain family background and  with an unstructured interview it really  is about the conversation and whatever  you personally think  drives success that is  used to measure interview performance a  structured interview is when you ask the  same questions to all of the interview  candidates ideally those are informed by  analyses of what types of factors  actually predict on the job's success so  i may think coming from iowa is a good  predictor of  on-the-job success for a given job but  that may have nothing to do with it if  you actually crunch the numbers so  structured interviews  ideally have data-driven structured  assessments whereby the candidate is  asked to do something job relevant the  reason and in general those tend to  reduce inequality because they put a  tamper on the personal biases that we  have  and our  elect keeps interviewers more  focusing on job relevant criteria  when interviews are unstructured  physical attractiveness race all these  things can can  run run crazy in interviewers  evaluations the one downside to having a  highly structured interview process and  i talk about this in the book a little  bit with consulting case interviews is  that if for some reason whatever  structure you've adopted let's say in  consulting they have these case  interviews where applicants are asked to  solve a business problem similar to one  that they might encounter on the job  and they have to go through  this very stylized script where if you  don't know the rules of this particular  type of interview interaction you're not  going to do well and in that setting  when a structured interview requires  mastery of insider codes that require  knowing an insider to to get ahead it  may not level the playing field  professor rivera you write wes  a law student from a working class  family  talked about recruiting events quote i  would generally stand over in the corner  with my friends or stand on by the bar  and then just get drunk so i didn't  really use that as a recruiting tool  maybe that would have been smart but i  felt like a tool bag i hate like  schmoozing when you go to cocktail  parties  i think that  the knowledge  what counts and what doesn't count in  the interview process  depends a lot on your connections and  your connections are influenced by your  background i also think that the knowing  that networking and schmoozing and even  chemistry in a job interview can carry  the day is a type of class-based  cultural knowledge that not everyone has  often working class students enter the  classroom enter universities enter  interviews with the opinion that what's  on their resume counts most and in  especially an elite job that's simply  not the case the networking is a huge  part of it and not only knowing that  you're supposed to do it but doing it  well can make or break a candidate  what do you teach here at northwestern  professor i teach leadership  what is the downside of  pedigree of elite students getting elite  jobs is there a downside i think that  there can be i think that one is that we  like to live in a society where at least  we believe that opportunity is at least  in theory open and when there are  processes in place like the ones i  document in the book that systematically  make it such that if you don't come from  one of these backgrounds your chances of  getting a top paying job are very small  it's not impossible it's just very very  difficult that threatens our our notion  of the american dream and  our being an open society but i think  for firms i think the most worrying part  is that there's some evidence to suggest  that students from the highest class  backgrounds as well as the most elite  schools really tend not to like these  jobs these jobs are really really  difficult they require lots of hours on  the job lots of mundane tasks and a lot  of people find them really really rough  but there's evidence to suggest that  elite students are and whether it's  class background or educational  background are actually worse fits for  the job than individuals for more modest  economic means or from less prestigious  schools so people who come from the  elite backgrounds tend to want to leave  these jobs earlier usually within the  one first one to two years of entering  before they've been there long enough to  pay back recruiting costs whereas some  of these jobs are meant as temporary  positions they're not necessarily meant  to take you all the way to partnership  but students from less elite backgrounds  tend to actually like the work more and  want to stay longer there's also some  research to suggest that individuals  from lower class backgrounds actually  have better  coping skills listening skills things  that are all asset and very demanding  client facing professions  are schools  guilty as well of  perpetuating this elite  system i think they are i don't  necessarily think it's intentional  i think that especially elite  universities have some pretty strong  incentives to keep this going  one is the fact that national rankings  especially for business and law schools  directly factor in starting salaries for  recent graduates into the rankings  so there is a and rankings have become  so important for universities both in  terms of their own status but in terms  of alumni donations things they can get  on  that can happen down the line so sending  as many students as possible to the  highest paying jobs benefits elite  universities  i think the second way that universities  are complicit is that the most elite  universities especially undergraduate  business school and law school is very  very top-ranked  they  allow these firms almost unfettered  access to their students and what that  does  is it creates a climate on campus where  these careers end up being a crucial  marker of self-worth for a lot of  students and so people end up going into  these careers probably in much greater  numbers than they would otherwise  because  the recruiting process that has been  described as literally taking over  campuses  for several months per year has made it  such that these are the most visible and  high status jobs available to students  we're sitting here at northwestern  university you teach at the kellogg  school of management yep your top five  aren't you we are we are and we send a  lot of our students to management  consulting and i'm a former management  consultant myself  but um yeah i think that universities  are  play a big role in this  how do you how do you fight this  i think that it requires two things the  first actually has to do with  universities one of the big reasons why  the playing field for elite jobs is so  skewed happens to be that universities  elite universities at both the  undergraduate and the graduate level  tend to use highly class biased  admission criteria  so the when you look at the student  population of a very top school whether  again it's at the undergraduate or in  business and law at the graduate level  these  programs tend to be dominated by  students from the most affluent  households so you're skewing the  population  to begin with  but when you look at firms that will  only recruit from those schools if they  have a class biased population from  which to recruit  their hiring practices are naturally  going to be class biased but then if you  add on top of that class biased hiring  practices like these firms have  privileging things like not only  school prestige but other things that  are correlated with socioeconomic status  of the applicant's parents such as  participation in high status  extracurricular activities unpaid  internship these very class-based  notions of polish and what it means to  be a well-spoken person  inside knowledge of the interview  process and so forth you result in what  you get is a system where there's a  double filter on socioeconomic status  does this lead to a racial bias at all  the racial biases are  part of the process so i think it's  important to disentangle race and  socioeconomic status because they are  very different and i think that this  hiring process has really strongly  disadvantaged students from low-income  backgrounds regardless of their race in  addition what happens in some of the  interviews  and some of the deliberations among  hiring committees is that people use  stereotypes as shortcuts to judge  candidates and especially with  african-american men there is a racial  stereotype that employers operate on  that people are less polished so you see  and it's very very clear that if an  applicant falters just a little bit  who's an african-american male on  something related to interpersonal  communication this triggers this larger  stereotype they're out of the pool i  watched how equivalent otherwise  equivalent applicants who were white men  who would make the exact same error and  they were kept in they were considered  to be coachable so i think that there  are subtle racial biases that happen  throughout this process but also in  addition to it  that that also create racial biases in  this process the other thing is is that  the schools from which these firms  recruit tend not to be the most racially  diverse schools to begin with so you  have the pipeline issue but then you  also again have the bias issue in terms  of what employers are doing to to  reinforce the inequalities we have in  society today professor rivera former  management consultant rivera  are you part of this elite system have  you been guilty of this yourself or  not trying to put you on the spot here  i'm just curious yeah i mean i never did  interviews from my old firm  but i've lived in this world for a long  time and  i think that  it is quite tempting to take a lot of  the shortcuts that these firms take and  that we take as humans in terms of you  like people more who are similar to you  if someone comes from a shared  background it's easier to relate to them  i think we're all guilty of that so i  don't think i'm i'm unique in that i  don't necessarily have any authority to  hire anyone so  you know not yet at least and i hope  that when i am in a position i'll put in  structured  protocols that will hopefully result in  a more fair is pedigree written for  management students is it written for  the general public it's written  primarily for sociologists actually  i got my phd in sociology and the impact  is really  intended to be for for scholars who are  interested in issues of inequality  if it has  also impacts for managers and  practitioners as well as mba students  i'm very very happy about that but the  intended audience was  was really sociologists but again with  any book it's really nice that anyone  reads it so if it gathers more public  appeal that's that's great too and  princeton press has put this book out  you use the word polished yep quite a  bit in this book yes what does it mean  to be polished so polish is what my  interviewees would talk about as they  use it interchangeably with presence and  communication skills these are soft  skills can you interact with a client  are you presentable  to a team to  to someone who's an executive and i  think that the definition of polish  varies from place to place if you look  at an advertising agency the type of  mannerisms and the type of behaviors  that are valued might be different from  an elite law firm likewise in academia  we have very different styles of  self-presentation than in the corporate  world but in this world it means a  couple of things it means being formal  without being too formal so having a  sense of relax of relaxation between you  and the person you're talking with  that's one of the reasons why a lot of  the black candidates are actually thrown  out as they're perceived to either be  too formal or too informal and that's a  judgment that happens through this lens  of racial stereotypes but so the first  thing is being  formal enough to be professional but not  too informal to be  immature the second which is really  important is in the interview context  taking the reins of the conversation so  a lot of people may think that in an  interview you're supposed to just  passively answer the questions the  interviewer asks you that's how you  would do an interview in many many many  different types of of occupations but  here it's called conversational  leadership taking the reins of  conversation the idea is that you're  trying to treat the interviewer as an  equal so rather than having them be you  have the position of power and you're  the person who wants the job you want to  create this into a conversation of  equals and there's a sociologist named  seamus khan who's at columbia who talks  about how that is a hallmark of economic  elites the ability to make anyone feel  at ease regardless of how  how high or how low they are  in term relative to you in the economic  hierarchy that sense of ease is  important and that is absolutely  essential and that type of communication  skill is different than if you went to  interview at say you wanted a job at a  factory if you tried to find  commonalities and create this sense of  equality your interviewer would not be  pleased  lauren rivera  if you don't go to an elite school  community college or a local local  campus  are there two strikes against you at  that point if you're going to a  community college and their chances are  you're not going to get through the door  if you are at a four-year university  that happens to be a non-elite  university say a public university  that's not a flagship or in some cases  some firms have a very very narrow  conception of elite and even a public  flagship would not be  prestigious enough for them the the key  there is to have a connection someone  inside a firm who can pass on your  resume and get you a look otherwise  these firms have so many applicants from  schools that they consider to be good  schools  and those are their words not mine i  think a lot of wonderful schools  in the country that are not on these  firms lists but  but unless you can get someone  get someone's attention through a  connection you're out  here's the book pedigree how elite  students get elite jobs  northwestern professor lauren rivera is  the author  is there a non-fiction author or book  you'd like to see featured on book tv  send us an email to booktv cspan.org  tweet us at booktv

