intended to be for for scholars who are  interested in issues of inequality  if it has  also impacts for managers and  practitioners as well as mba students  i'm very very happy about that but the  intended audience was  was really sociologists but again with  any book it's really nice that anyone  reads it so if it gathers more public  appeal that's that's great too and  princeton press has put this book out  you use the word polished yep quite a  bit in this book yes what does it mean  to be polished so polish is what my  interviewees would talk about as they  use it interchangeably with presence and  communication skills these are soft  skills can you interact with a client  are you presentable  to a team to  to someone who's an executive and i  think that the definition of polish  varies from place to place if you look  at an advertising agency the type of  mannerisms and the type of behaviors  that are valued might be different from  an elite law firm likewise in academia  we have very different styles of  self-presentation than in the corporate  world but in this world it means a  couple of things it means being formal  without being too formal so having a  sense of relax of relaxation between you  and the person you're talking with  that's one of the reasons why a lot of  the black candidates are actually thrown  out as they're perceived to either be  too formal or too informal and that's a  judgment that happens through this lens  of racial stereotypes but so the first  thing is being  formal enough to be professional but not  too informal to be  immature the second which is really  important is in the interview context  taking the reins of the conversation so  a lot of people may think that in an  interview you're supposed to just  passively answer the questions the  interviewer asks you that's how you  would do an interview in many many many  different types of of occupations but  here it's called conversational  leadership taking the reins of  conversation the idea is that you're  trying to treat the interviewer as an  equal so rather than having them be you  have the position of power and you're  the person who wants the job you want to  create this into a conversation of  equals and there's a sociologist named  seamus khan who's at columbia who talks  about how that is a hallmark of economic  elites the ability to make anyone feel  at ease regardless of how  how high or how low they are  in term relative to you in the economic  hierarchy that sense of ease is  important and that is absolutely  essential and that type of communication  skill is different than if you went to  interview at say you wanted a job at a  factory if you tried to find  commonalities and create this sense of  equality your interviewer would not be  pleased  lauren rivera  if you don't go to an elite school  community college or a local local  campus  are there two strikes against you at  that point if you're going to a  community college and their chances are  you're not going to get through the door  if you are at a four-year university  that happens to be a non-elite  university say a public university  that's not a flagship or in some cases  some firms have a very very narrow  conception of elite and even a public  flagship would not be  prestigious enough for them the the key  there is to have a connection someone  inside a firm who can pass on your  resume and get you a look otherwise  these firms have so many applicants from  schools that they consider to be good  schools  and those are their words not mine i  think a lot of wonderful schools  in the country that are not on these  firms lists but  but unless you can get someone  get someone's attention through a  connection you're out  here's the book pedigree how elite  students get elite jobs  northwestern professor lauren rivera is  the author  is there a non-fiction author or book  you'd like to see featured on book tv  send us an email to booktv cspan.org  tweet us at booktv
 

